window.EXAM_DESK_DATA = {"schemaVersion":1,"version":"a82fa1d5b1c53acd","exams":[{"id":"2026-english-one","title":"2026 考研英语一","year":2026,"kind":"真题","durationMinutes":180,"totalScore":100,"source":{"paper":"2026年考研英语一真题.pdf","analysis":"2026年解析及复习思路（全）.pdf","note":"英文原题据用户提供的试卷整理；答案参考用户提供的解析核对。中文译文、词义和简析由模型整理，仅供学习，不是官方译文或评分标准。"},"sections":[{"id":"cloze","title":"英语知识运用","subtitle":"1–20题 · 10分","type":"cloze","directions":"Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","paragraphs":[{"en":"Advances in artificial intelligence (AI) are rapidly changing every aspect of human life. The world of AI is buzzing with an exciting potential to improve and enrich our lives. [[1]], AI also has the potential hazard of [[2]] our experiences in ways we might find difficult to control. One such [[3]] is how we understand and experience beauty.","zh":"人工智能（AI）的进步正在迅速改变人类生活的方方面面。人工智能领域充满令人兴奋的潜力，有望改善并丰富我们的生活。不过，人工智能也可能以我们难以控制的方式主导我们的体验，带来潜在风险。其中一个这样的领域，就是我们如何理解和体验美。"},{"en":"AI can be a collaborative tool in a wide range of creative endeavors. [[4]] human creativity and AI algorithms can lead to unique artistic [[5]] that are beautiful to the human eye. These collaborations are likely to become increasingly common. [[6]], as convenient and provocative, AI enables virtual try-on experiences where you can virtually [[7]] makeup, hairstyles, clothing, and even cosmetic procedures [[8]] making any physical changes. Individuals can now experiment with different looks and [[9]] their preferences, potentially expanding the range of beauty ideals.","zh":"人工智能可以成为广泛创作活动中的协作工具。将人类创造力与人工智能算法相结合，可以产生在人类眼中很美的独特艺术成果。此类合作可能会越来越普遍。例如，人工智能提供方便而能激发思考的虚拟试用体验，让你在不做任何实际改变之前，虚拟尝试化妆、发型、服装，甚至美容手术。如今，人们可以尝试不同形象，探索自己的偏好，从而可能拓宽理想美的范围。"},{"en":"AI algorithms can [[10]] facial features and skin conditions to provide personalized beauty recommendations. This [[11]] approach aims to cater to individual preferences and enhance the concept of beauty tailored to each person's unique characteristics. [[12]], AI can be a fun vehicle for self-discovery.","zh":"人工智能算法可以分析面部特征和皮肤状况，从而提供个性化美容建议。这种定制方式旨在迎合个人偏好，并强化一种根据每个人独特特点量身打造的美的观念。这样一来，人工智能就可以成为一种有趣的自我探索工具。"},{"en":"While AI offers exciting possibilities, it also raises ethical [[13]]. There is a risk of deepening societal beauty [[14]] and perpetuating unattainable beauty standards. [[15]], AI-powered beauty filters and editing tools can lead to distorted self-perception and [[16]] body dissatisfaction. As summarized in a recent post on “The Hidden Dangers of Online Beauty Filters”, [[17]] on this technology for social presentation can cause harm [[18]] body image issues, lower self-esteem, and social anxiety.","zh":"尽管人工智能带来令人兴奋的可能性，它也引发伦理方面的担忧。它可能加剧社会中的容貌压力，并延续那些难以达到的审美标准。此外，人工智能美颜滤镜和编辑工具可能扭曲自我认知，并加剧人们对身体的不满。正如最近一篇《网络美颜滤镜的隐患》所总结的那样，依赖这种技术来展示自己的社交形象，可能造成身体形象问题、自尊降低和社交焦虑等伤害。"},{"en":"It's important to note that while AI can enhance our [[19]] of beauty, it should not [[20]] the genuine human experience and the emotional connections we derive from seeing the beauty in each other.","zh":"需要注意的是，虽然人工智能可以提高我们欣赏美的能力，但它不应取代真实的人类体验，以及我们从发现彼此之美中获得的情感联系。"}],"notes":[{"text":"One such [[3]] is how we understand and experience beauty.","translation":"其中一个这样的领域，就是我们如何理解和体验美。","hint":"主干是 One such area is…；how 引导表语从句，整体说明这一领域是什么。"},{"text":"This [[11]] approach aims to cater to individual preferences and enhance the concept of beauty tailored to each person's unique characteristics.","translation":"这种定制方式旨在迎合个人偏好，并强化根据每个人独特特点量身打造的美的观念。","hint":"aims to 后连接 cater 和 enhance 两个并列动作；tailored to…是过去分词短语，修饰 beauty。"},{"text":"It's important to note that while AI can enhance our [[19]] of beauty, it should not [[20]] the genuine human experience and the emotional connections we derive from seeing the beauty in each other.","translation":"需要注意的是，虽然人工智能可以提高我们欣赏美的能力，但它不应取代真实的人类体验，以及我们从发现彼此之美中获得的情感联系。","hint":"It 是形式主语，to note…是真正主语；that 引出所注意的内容，其中 while 表让步，we derive…修饰 connections。"}],"questions":[{"number":1,"type":"choice","stem":"第 1 空","stemZh":"第 1 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Still","meaning":"虽然如此；不过"},{"key":"B","text":"Therefore","meaning":"因此；所以"},{"key":"C","text":"Afterward","meaning":"后来；之后"},{"key":"D","text":"Instead","meaning":"反而；代替"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"前文谈积极潜力，空后转向潜在风险，形成转折；Still 表示“虽然如此；不过”。","points":0.5},{"number":2,"type":"choice","stem":"第 2 空","stemZh":"第 2 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"reviewing","meaning":"回顾；审查"},{"key":"B","text":"narrating","meaning":"叙述；讲述"},{"key":"C","text":"ignoring","meaning":"忽视；不理会"},{"key":"D","text":"dominating","meaning":"支配；主导"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"后文 difficult to control（难以控制）提示失去自主权，因此选 dominating（主导体验）。reviewing 等词不能体现这种风险。","points":0.5},{"number":3,"type":"choice","stem":"第 3 空","stemZh":"第 3 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"reason","meaning":"原因；理由"},{"key":"B","text":"area","meaning":"领域；方面；区域"},{"key":"C","text":"clue","meaning":"线索；提示"},{"key":"D","text":"belief","meaning":"信念；看法"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"空后说明“理解和体验美”这一方面，area 可表示“领域”。","points":0.5},{"number":4,"type":"choice","stem":"第 4 空","stemZh":"第 4 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Balancing","meaning":"平衡；使均衡"},{"key":"B","text":"Distinguishing","meaning":"区分；辨别"},{"key":"C","text":"Combining","meaning":"结合；联合"},{"key":"D","text":"Introducing","meaning":"介绍；引入"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"将人的创造力与 AI 算法结合才能形成协作成果；Combining A and B 表示“将 A 与 B 结合”。","points":0.5},{"number":5,"type":"choice","stem":"第 5 空","stemZh":"第 5 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"prospects","meaning":"前景；可能性"},{"key":"B","text":"outcomes","meaning":"结果；成果"},{"key":"C","text":"ambitions","meaning":"抱负；雄心"},{"key":"D","text":"sentiments","meaning":"情绪；情感；观点"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"lead to 后需要创作所产生的“成果”，outcomes 与 artistic 及 beautiful 呼应。","points":0.5},{"number":6,"type":"choice","stem":"第 6 空","stemZh":"第 6 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"At first","meaning":"起初；首先"},{"key":"B","text":"By comparison","meaning":"相比之下"},{"key":"C","text":"For instance","meaning":"例如"},{"key":"D","text":"In general","meaning":"总的来说；通常"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"后文虚拟试妆是 AI 创造性用途的具体例子，For instance 引出举例。","points":0.5},{"number":7,"type":"choice","stem":"第 7 空","stemZh":"第 7 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"test","meaning":"测试；试用"},{"key":"B","text":"copy","meaning":"复制；模仿"},{"key":"C","text":"link","meaning":"连接；联系"},{"key":"D","text":"save","meaning":"保存；节省；挽救"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"virtual try-on 与尝试妆容、发型等相对应，test 在此表示“试用”。","points":0.5},{"number":8,"type":"choice","stem":"第 8 空","stemZh":"第 8 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"upon","meaning":"在……上；一……就……"},{"key":"B","text":"beyond","meaning":"超出；在……之外"},{"key":"C","text":"through","meaning":"通过；穿过"},{"key":"D","text":"before","meaning":"在……之前"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"虚拟尝试发生在真正改变外观之前，before 与 virtual try-on 的优势相符。","points":0.5},{"number":9,"type":"choice","stem":"第 9 空","stemZh":"第 9 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"explore","meaning":"探索；探究"},{"key":"B","text":"recall","meaning":"回忆；召回"},{"key":"C","text":"simplify","meaning":"简化"},{"key":"D","text":"cherish","meaning":"珍视；珍爱"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"experiment with different looks 后接探索个人偏好，explore 与自我发现的语境相符。","points":0.5},{"number":10,"type":"choice","stem":"第 10 空","stemZh":"第 10 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"recover","meaning":"恢复；找回"},{"key":"B","text":"arrange","meaning":"安排；整理"},{"key":"C","text":"reserve","meaning":"预订；保留"},{"key":"D","text":"analyze","meaning":"分析"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"为了给出个性化建议，算法先“分析”面部特征及皮肤状况，选 analyze。","points":0.5},{"number":11,"type":"choice","stem":"第 11 空","stemZh":"第 11 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"localized","meaning":"局部化的；本地化的"},{"key":"B","text":"normalized","meaning":"标准化的；正常化的"},{"key":"C","text":"randomized","meaning":"随机化的"},{"key":"D","text":"customized","meaning":"定制的；个性化的"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"personalized（个性化）与 tailored to each person（为个人量身打造）都指向定制，因此选 customized。","points":0.5},{"number":12,"type":"choice","stem":"第 12 空","stemZh":"第 12 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"At best","meaning":"充其量；至多"},{"key":"B","text":"To the contrary","meaning":"相反"},{"key":"C","text":"By definition","meaning":"按照定义"},{"key":"D","text":"In this way","meaning":"以这种方式；这样一来"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"上文解释个性化建议如何实现，In this way 承接方法并引出自我探索这一结果。","points":0.5},{"number":13,"type":"choice","stem":"第 13 空","stemZh":"第 13 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"divisions","meaning":"分歧；划分"},{"key":"B","text":"expectations","meaning":"期待；预期"},{"key":"C","text":"concerns","meaning":"担忧；关切"},{"key":"D","text":"values","meaning":"价值观；价值"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"While 引出与积极可能性相对的伦理问题，ethical concerns 表示“伦理担忧”。","points":0.5},{"number":14,"type":"choice","stem":"第 14 空","stemZh":"第 14 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"pressures","meaning":"压力"},{"key":"B","text":"mysteries","meaning":"谜；神秘事物"},{"key":"C","text":"understandings","meaning":"理解；认识"},{"key":"D","text":"suspicions","meaning":"怀疑；猜疑"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"难以达到的审美标准会加剧容貌压力，beauty pressures 符合负面语境。","points":0.5},{"number":15,"type":"choice","stem":"第 15 空","stemZh":"第 15 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Approximately","meaning":"大约；近似地"},{"key":"B","text":"Additionally","meaning":"此外；另外"},{"key":"C","text":"Alternatively","meaning":"或者；作为另一种选择"},{"key":"D","text":"Accidentally","meaning":"偶然地；意外地"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"后一句继续补充 AI 的另一项风险，Additionally 表并列递进。","points":0.5},{"number":16,"type":"choice","stem":"第 16 空","stemZh":"第 16 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"deal with","meaning":"处理；应对"},{"key":"B","text":"result from","meaning":"由……造成"},{"key":"C","text":"contribute to","meaning":"促成；导致；有助于"},{"key":"D","text":"focus on","meaning":"关注；聚焦于"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"滤镜和编辑工具会导致身体不满，contribute to 表示“促成；导致”。","points":0.5},{"number":17,"type":"choice","stem":"第 17 空","stemZh":"第 17 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"starting","meaning":"开始"},{"key":"B","text":"checking","meaning":"检查；核实"},{"key":"C","text":"relying","meaning":"依赖；依靠"},{"key":"D","text":"working","meaning":"工作；运转"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"前文谈美颜工具导致自我认知扭曲，此处进一步说“依赖这种技术展示自己”会造成伤害，因此选 relying，与 on 搭配。","points":0.5},{"number":18,"type":"choice","stem":"第 18 空","stemZh":"第 18 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"apart from","meaning":"除……以外"},{"key":"B","text":"such as","meaning":"例如；诸如"},{"key":"C","text":"regardless of","meaning":"不管；不顾"},{"key":"D","text":"prior to","meaning":"在……之前"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"harm 后列出身体形象问题、自尊降低和社交焦虑，都是伤害的具体例子，因此用 such as 引出。","points":0.5},{"number":19,"type":"choice","stem":"第 19 空","stemZh":"第 19 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"imitation","meaning":"模仿；仿制"},{"key":"B","text":"appreciation","meaning":"欣赏；鉴赏；感激"},{"key":"C","text":"preservation","meaning":"保护；保存"},{"key":"D","text":"consumption","meaning":"消费；消耗"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"全文讨论理解与体验美，结尾归纳 AI 能提升对美的欣赏，选 appreciation。","points":0.5},{"number":20,"type":"choice","stem":"第 20 空","stemZh":"第 20 空","options":[{"key":"A","text":"replace","meaning":"取代；更换"},{"key":"B","text":"seize","meaning":"抓住；夺取"},{"key":"C","text":"share","meaning":"分享；共有"},{"key":"D","text":"reflect","meaning":"反映；反思"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"should not 后强调 AI 的边界：它不应“取代”真人体验与情感联系，选 replace。","points":0.5}],"image":null},{"id":"reading-1","title":"Reading · Text 1","subtitle":"21–25题 · 10分","type":"reading","directions":"Read the following text. Answer the questions after the text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET.","paragraphs":[{"en":"For thousands of years, donkeys have been critical for propelling human civilizations forward. They’ve helped pull wheeled vehicles, carry travelers and move goods across the world. But where and when these animals first became intertwined with humans has been a mystery. Now, researchers have used genomes of over 200 donkeys to trace their domestication back to a single event around 7,000 years ago in East Africa—about 3,000 years before humans tamed horses. The team published their findings in the journal Science this month.","zh":"数千年来，驴一直对推动人类文明进步至关重要。它们帮助拉车、载客，并将货物运往世界各地。但这些动物最初在何时、何地与人类产生紧密联系，一直是个谜。如今，研究人员利用两百多头驴的基因组，将驴的驯化追溯到大约七千年前东非发生的一次事件——比人类驯服马约早三千年。研究团队本月在《科学》杂志上发表了研究结果。"},{"en":"“Through their DNA, the animals are telling their history themselves,” co-author Samantha Brooks, an equine researcher at the University of Florida, says in a statement. “We usually only get the human's side of history through written accounts, but of course written history does not always record exactly how something happened. Looking at these DNA sequences, we get a biological testimony to the environment these animals lived in and the experiences they survived.”","zh":"合著者、佛罗里达大学马科动物研究人员萨曼莎·布鲁克斯在声明中说：“这些动物正通过自身的 DNA 讲述自己的历史。我们通常只能通过文字记录了解人类一方的历史，但文字记录当然并不总能准确反映事情究竟如何发生。通过研究这些 DNA 序列，我们能获得生物学证据，了解这些动物生活过的环境以及它们历经的遭遇。”"},{"en":"The researchers examined 207 genomes from modern donkeys living in 31 countries across the globe. They also looked at genomes from 15 wild equids and 31 earlier donkeys that lived between about 4,000 and 100 years ago. The team reconstructed the animals' evolutionary tree and used computer models to pinpoint the domestication event when herders in Kenya and the Horn of Africa tamed wild asses. They then traced how the animals spread across the rest of the continent into Europe and Asia about 2,500 years later.","zh":"研究人员考察了来自全球三十一个国家的现代驴的二百零七份基因组。他们还研究了十五匹野生马科动物，以及三十一头生活在大约四千年前至一百年前的古代驴的基因组。团队重建了这些动物的进化树，并利用计算机模型确定了驯化事件：肯尼亚和非洲之角的牧民驯服了野驴。随后，他们追踪这些动物如何在大约两千五百年后扩散到非洲大陆其余地区，并进入欧洲和亚洲。"},{"en":"Though it's still unclear why the original domestication happened, Science News' Freda Kreier reports that the event coincided with the Sahara growing larger and drier. “Donkeys are champions when it comes to carrying stuff and are good at going at deserts,” co-author Ludovic Orlando, an evolutionary biologist at Paul Sabin University in France, tells the publication. Prehistoric humans may have tamed donkeys to help navigate the expanding Sahara.","zh":"尽管最初发生驯化的原因尚不清楚，《科学新闻》的弗丽达·克赖尔报道说，这一事件恰逢撒哈拉沙漠变得更大、更干燥。合著者、法国保罗·萨宾大学进化生物学家吕多维克·奥兰多告诉该刊：“说到驮东西，驴堪称冠军，而且它们擅长在沙漠中行进。”史前人类可能为了穿越不断扩大的撒哈拉沙漠而驯服驴。"},{"en":"Researchers say these findings could help put donkeys in the spotlight. The animals could benefit from more research: Currently, there are no published genomes from donkeys located south of the Equator in Africa. But understanding where the animals were first domesticated could guide archaeologists to a narrow region to search for insights about the original tamed donkeys.","zh":"研究人员说，这些发现有助于让驴受到更多关注。这些动物可以从更多研究中受益：目前，非洲赤道以南地区的驴还没有已发表的基因组。不过，了解驴最初在哪里被驯化，可以引导考古学家将搜寻范围缩小到一片区域，从而深入了解最早的家驴。"},{"en":"Not only does understanding the equines’ genetic makeup help reveal their contribution to human history, but it also might improve their management in the future, as climate change alters the planet's environment, write the authors.","zh":"作者写道，了解这些马科动物的基因构成，不仅有助于揭示它们对人类历史的贡献，而且在气候变化改变地球环境的背景下，还可能改善未来对它们的管理。"}],"notes":[{"text":"But where and when these animals first became intertwined with humans has been a mystery.","translation":"但这些动物最初在何时、何地与人类产生紧密联系，一直是个谜。","hint":"where and when…是整个名词性从句，作主语；谓语是 has been，表语是 a mystery。不要把 humans 当成主语。"},{"text":"Not only does understanding the equines’ genetic makeup help reveal their contribution to human history, but it also might improve their management in the future, as climate change alters the planet's environment, write the authors.","translation":"作者写道，了解马科动物的基因构成不仅有助于揭示其对人类历史的贡献，而且在气候变化改变地球环境时，还可能改善未来对它们的管理。","hint":"Not only 置于句首引起部分倒装；主语是 understanding…这个动名词短语，does 后接 help 原形。but it also…与前半句并列。"}],"questions":[{"number":21,"type":"choice","stem":"What can be learned about donkeys from Paragraph 1?","stemZh":"从第一段可以了解到关于驴的什么信息？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"They seemed mysterious to human ancestors.","meaning":"它们在人类祖先眼中显得神秘。seem：显得；ancestor：祖先。"},{"key":"B","text":"They underwent multiple domestication events.","meaning":"它们经历了多次驯化事件。undergo：经历；multiple：多次的；domestication：驯化。"},{"key":"C","text":"They were tamed at an earlier time than horses.","meaning":"它们比马更早被驯服。tame：驯服；earlier：更早的。"},{"key":"D","text":"They were vividly portrayed by ancient travelers.","meaning":"它们被古代旅行者生动地描绘。vividly：生动地；portray：描绘。"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"第一段明确说驴的驯化发生在约七千年前，比驯马早约三千年；C 是直接同义改写。","points":2},{"number":22,"type":"choice","stem":"What message is conveyed in Brooks' statement?","stemZh":"布鲁克斯的声明传达了什么信息？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The earliest habitats of donkeys are hardly traceable.","meaning":"驴最早的栖息地几乎无法追溯。habitat：栖息地；hardly：几乎不；traceable：可追溯的。"},{"key":"B","text":"It is increasingly easy to read donkeys' DNA sequences.","meaning":"解读驴的 DNA 序列变得越来越容易。increasingly：越来越；sequence：序列。"},{"key":"C","text":"Written accounts contain vital clues for donkey research.","meaning":"文字记录含有驴研究的重要线索。written account：文字记录；vital：重要的；clue：线索。"},{"key":"D","text":"Genetic analysis offers insight into the history of donkeys.","meaning":"基因分析让人深入了解驴的历史。genetic analysis：基因分析；insight：深入理解。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"布鲁克斯强调 DNA 能提供关于环境与经历的生物学证据，弥补人类文字记载的局限；D 概括这一观点。","points":2},{"number":23,"type":"choice","stem":"In their study, the researchers investigated how donkeys","stemZh":"研究人员在研究中考察了驴如何……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"dispersed widely in the world.","meaning":"广泛分布到世界各地。disperse：扩散；散布。"},{"key":"B","text":"survived with the help of herders.","meaning":"在牧民的帮助下生存。survive：存活；herder：牧民。"},{"key":"C","text":"developed certain behavioral traits.","meaning":"形成某些行为特征。behavioral trait：行为特征。"},{"key":"D","text":"adapted to the changing environment.","meaning":"适应不断变化的环境。adapt to：适应。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"第三段末句追踪驴如何 spread（扩散）到欧洲和亚洲，A 中 dispersed（散布）与之同义。","points":2},{"number":24,"type":"choice","stem":"As to why the original domestication of donkeys happened, Orlando","stemZh":"关于驴最初为何被驯化，奥兰多……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"challenges conventional ideas.","meaning":"挑战传统观念。challenge：质疑；conventional：传统的。"},{"key":"B","text":"provides a possible explanation.","meaning":"提供一种可能的解释。provide：提供；explanation：解释。"},{"key":"C","text":"calls for evidence from the Sahara.","meaning":"呼吁寻找来自撒哈拉的证据。call for：呼吁；evidence：证据。"},{"key":"D","text":"holds a different view from Kreier.","meaning":"与克赖尔持不同观点。hold a view：持有观点。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"第四段提到沙漠扩大、驴擅长驮运和沙漠行进，并用 may have…提出驯化的可能原因；选 B，不是证实原因。","points":2},{"number":25,"type":"choice","stem":"The authors think that their research could help with","stemZh":"作者认为，他们的研究有助于……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"greater protection of wildlife.","meaning":"更好地保护野生动物。protection：保护；wildlife：野生动物。"},{"key":"B","text":"better management of donkeys.","meaning":"更好地管理驴。management：管理。"},{"key":"C","text":"recovering early types of donkeys.","meaning":"恢复早期类型的驴。recover：恢复；early type：早期类型。"},{"key":"D","text":"raising awareness of climate change.","meaning":"提高气候变化意识。raise awareness：提高认识。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"末段明确说研究可能 improve their management in the future，B 直接对应。","points":2}],"image":null},{"id":"reading-2","title":"Reading · Text 2","subtitle":"26–30题 · 10分","type":"reading","directions":"Read the following text. Answer the questions after the text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET.","paragraphs":[{"en":"There's no business like show business—but in Los Angeles, it feels like there's no business at all.","zh":"没有哪种生意能比得上演艺业——但在洛杉矶，感觉却像是根本没有生意可做。"},{"en":"If that sounds melodramatic, consider this: The Art Directors Guild, a labor union representing about 3,000 film workers, has suspended a training program and issued a statement explaining that “we cannot in good conscience encourage you to pursue our profession”. This is a reaction to Hollywood's decline, which is reaching a critical point for the industry and Southern California.","zh":"如果这听起来太夸张，请看看这一事实：代表约三千名影视工作者的工会——艺术指导工会——已经暂停了一项培训计划，并发表声明解释说：“我们无法昧着良心鼓励你们从事我们这一行。”这是对好莱坞衰退的回应，而这种衰退对整个行业和南加州而言，正到达一个关键节点。"},{"en":"Production has been slipping away from Hollywood since the 1950s, but the effects have never been more apparent than at present. Other regions in the United States, Canada and Europe have steadily increased incentives to attract TV shows and movies, leaving California in the dust. Georgia offers up to 30% in transferable tax credits on film and TV production costs, plus an additional 10% increase on the base tax credit if the project includes a Georgia promotional logo.","zh":"影视制作自二十世纪五十年代以来就在不断从好莱坞流走，但这种影响从未像现在这样明显。美国其他地区、加拿大和欧洲持续增加激励措施，以吸引电视节目和电影制作，把加州远远甩在后面。佐治亚州对影视制作成本提供最高百分之三十的可转让税收抵免；如果作品加入佐治亚州的推广标识，还可在基础税收抵免上再增加百分之十。"},{"en":"Even as California lost a huge volume of production to other locations, there was still plenty of film production taking place in Los Angeles before this year. We were kept afloat by “peak TV”, the glut of content that was required by the explosion of streaming services.","zh":"尽管加州有大量制作业务流向其他地方，但在今年以前，洛杉矶仍有大量影视制作活动。我们靠“电视巅峰期”维持着运转——流媒体服务爆发式增长，需要海量内容。"},{"en":"But 2022 was the peak of peak TV. Back then platforms such as Netflix, Amazon and Apple TV hemorrhaged billions of dollars to generate content to attract new subscribers, resulting in 633 scripted series being released that year. As the streamers’ emphasis changed from subscriber growth to profitability, prices for the services went up and the number of new shows went down to 481 released in 2023, with the number expected to dip into the 300s within a few years.","zh":"然而，二〇二二年已经是电视巅峰期的顶峰。当时，奈飞、亚马逊和 Apple TV 等平台为制作内容、吸引新订阅用户而投入数十亿美元，结果那一年推出了六百三十三部有剧本的剧集。随着流媒体平台的重点从增加订阅用户转向盈利，服务价格上升，而新剧数量下降至二〇二三年的四百八十一部；预计几年内还会降到三百多部。"},{"en":"If productions in Southern California dip below a critical level for too long, the industry's essential talent will drift away along with enormous sums of revenue. Persuading studios to film here would become much more challenging if we couldn't offer a deep bench of local film workers, on-screen talent and local businesses that support the entertainment industry.","zh":"如果南加州的影视制作量长期低于临界水平，这个行业不可或缺的人才将会流失，巨额收入也会随之流走。如果我们无法提供充足的本地影视工作人员、演员以及支持娱乐业的本地企业，说服制片公司来这里拍摄将会困难得多。"},{"en":"That's why the California Film Commission and its Los Angeles counterpart, Film LA, now should act, before it's too late. These agencies and other government bodies should dramatically improve incentives to keep our current shows and attract new productions to Los Angeles. Let's go on with the show…and make sure the show doesn't go on without us.","zh":"因此，加州电影委员会和与其对应的洛杉矶机构 Film LA 现在就应该行动，趁一切还来得及。这些机构与其他政府部门应该大幅改善激励措施，以留住现有节目，并吸引新的制作项目来到洛杉矶。让演出继续吧……而且要确保演出不会撇下我们继续进行。"}],"notes":[{"text":"As the streamers’ emphasis changed from subscriber growth to profitability, prices for the services went up and the number of new shows went down to 481 released in 2023, with the number expected to dip into the 300s within a few years.","translation":"随着流媒体平台重点从订阅用户增长转向盈利，服务价格上涨，二〇二三年新剧数量下降到四百八十一部，预计几年内还会降至三百多部。","hint":"As 从句说明背景及原因；主句为 prices went up 和 the number went down 两部分；with…expected to…补充对数量的预测。"},{"text":"Persuading studios to film here would become much more challenging if we couldn't offer a deep bench of local film workers, on-screen talent and local businesses that support the entertainment industry.","translation":"如果我们无法提供充足的本地影视工作者、演员及支持娱乐业的本地企业，说服制片公司来此拍摄就会困难得多。","hint":"Persuading studios to film here 是动名词短语作主语；if 引导条件从句，a deep bench 比喻充足的人才或资源储备。"}],"questions":[{"number":26,"type":"choice","stem":"The Art Directors Guild's statement reveals____","stemZh":"艺术指导工会的声明揭示了……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"people's reduced interest in film.","meaning":"人们对电影的兴趣下降。reduced interest：减弱的兴趣。"},{"key":"B","text":"film workers' nostalgia for the past.","meaning":"影视工作者怀念过去。nostalgia：怀旧；怀念。"},{"key":"C","text":"the appeal of Southern California.","meaning":"南加州的吸引力。appeal：吸引力。"},{"key":"D","text":"the gloomy situation of Hollywood.","meaning":"好莱坞的低迷处境。gloomy：暗淡的；低迷的。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"暂停培训并劝人不要入行，体现好莱坞行业处境严峻；第二段末的 decline 是关键信号，选 D。","points":2},{"number":27,"type":"choice","stem":"The example of Georgia is used to illustrate the efforts to____","stemZh":"佐治亚州的例子用于说明为……所作的努力。","options":[{"key":"A","text":"lure production with tax incentives.","meaning":"用税收优惠吸引制作项目。lure：吸引；tax incentive：税收激励。"},{"key":"B","text":"drive improvements in film facilities.","meaning":"推动改善影视设施。drive：推动；facility：设施。"},{"key":"C","text":"stimulate competition among states.","meaning":"激发各州之间的竞争。stimulate：激发；competition：竞争。"},{"key":"D","text":"collect funds for film and TV making.","meaning":"为影视制作筹集资金。collect funds：筹集资金。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"第三段先说其他地区加大激励吸引影视制作，再以佐治亚税收抵免为例；A 对应 attract…incentives。","points":2},{"number":28,"type":"choice","stem":"Peak TV passed its peak as____","stemZh":"电视巅峰期之所以过去，是因为……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"streamers lost their technical advantages.","meaning":"流媒体平台丧失了技术优势。technical advantage：技术优势。"},{"key":"B","text":"streamers changed their strategic priorities.","meaning":"流媒体平台改变了战略重点。strategic priority：战略重点。"},{"key":"C","text":"subscribers grew wary of large platforms.","meaning":"订阅用户开始警惕大平台。grow wary of：变得警惕。"},{"key":"D","text":"subscribers were unhappy with new shows.","meaning":"订阅用户对新节目不满意。unhappy with：对……不满意。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"第五段 As 引出原因：平台重点由 subscriber growth 转向 profitability；B 中战略重点是 emphasis 的改写。","points":2},{"number":29,"type":"choice","stem":"According to Paragraph 6, California’s entertainment industry might face____","stemZh":"根据第六段，加州娱乐业可能面临……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"a decline in product quality.","meaning":"产品质量下降。decline：下降。"},{"key":"B","text":"a demand for foreign talent.","meaning":"对外国人才的需求。demand for：对……的需求。"},{"key":"C","text":"a brain drain to other places.","meaning":"人才流失到其他地方。brain drain：人才外流。"},{"key":"D","text":"a dramatic rise in labor costs.","meaning":"劳动力成本急剧上升。dramatic rise：急剧增加。"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"第六段 essential talent will drift away 指人才将外流；C 中 brain drain 概括这一风险。","points":2},{"number":30,"type":"choice","stem":"The author concludes the text by emphasizing that California should strive to____","stemZh":"作者在结尾强调，加州应该努力……","options":[{"key":"A","text":"maintain its position in the industry.","meaning":"保持在行业中的地位。maintain：维持；position：地位。"},{"key":"B","text":"attract more investment than it had.","meaning":"吸引比过去更多的投资。investment：投资。"},{"key":"C","text":"pursue a higher standard of production.","meaning":"追求更高的制作标准。pursue：追求；standard：标准。"},{"key":"D","text":"strengthen coordination with other states.","meaning":"加强与其他州的协调。strengthen：加强；coordination：协调。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"最后呼吁增加激励、留住节目与吸引新项目，根本目的是维持加州在影视业中的地位；A 概括全段。","points":2}],"image":null},{"id":"reading-3","title":"Reading · Text 3","subtitle":"31–35题 · 10分","type":"reading","directions":"Read the following text. Answer the questions after the text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET.","paragraphs":[{"en":"The pioneers of wireless saw it as a gift to all the people. Sir John Reith said that it would end “isolation of the spirit” and rejoiced: “It does not matter how many thousands may be listening, there is always enough for others. . . the genius and the fool, the wealthy and the poor listen simultaneously.”","zh":"无线广播的先驱把它看作送给所有人的礼物。约翰·里斯爵士说，它将结束“精神上的孤立”，并欣喜地表示：“无论有成千上万的多少人在收听，总有足够的内容供其他人收听……天才与愚人、富人与穷人，都能同时收听。”"},{"en":"Between two great wars this technological innovation built a new kind of national consciousness. Opening this week, a book and exhibition curated by Beatty Rubens at the Bodleian in Oxford records how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939. She draws on letters, diaries and fiction, and a 1939 field notebook of verbatim audience research by Winifred Gill.","zh":"在两次世界大战之间，这项技术创新塑造了一种新的国家共同意识。本周，一本书及一场由比蒂·鲁本斯策划的展览在牛津的博德利图书馆推出，记录了广播在1922年至1939年间如何改变日常生活。她采用的资料包括书信、日记、小说，以及温妮弗雷德·吉尔在1939年开展听众调查时逐字记录受访者话语的实地笔记。"},{"en":"There's fun in testimonies of people enjoying the sheer newness. A cartoon mocks a group failing to converse because they're all in headphones. People report that broadcast music made workmen whistle new tunes. A woman says there have been fewer street fights since the arrival of the wireless but also less stopping and “talking on the brush handle”.","zh":"那些享受广播纯粹新鲜感的人留下的见证颇有趣味。一幅漫画嘲讽了一群无法交谈的人，因为他们全都戴着耳机。有人说，广播中的音乐让工人们吹起了新曲子。一位女士说，自从有了无线广播，街头打架少了，但人们停下脚步、“倚着扫帚柄聊天”的时候也少了。"},{"en":"By and large the wireless was welcome. I loved the man from the Thirties research who found that wireless suddenly offered “a lot of variety. things I thought I'd never be interested in…ice hockey perhaps”. True: for more than 80 pre-digital years, linear speech broadcasting brought the gift of serendipity, random enlivening of a car journey or dull manual task. In my own book about radio I recorded how, on one drive: “I caught up with the news, learnt some 17th-century history, and was startlingly educated by an unpretentious programme on the history of the stethoscope.”","zh":"总的来说，无线广播受到欢迎。我很喜欢20世纪30年代调查中的那位男士：他发现广播突然提供了“很多不同的东西，一些我原以为自己永远不会感兴趣的东西……也许是冰球”。的确，在数字时代到来之前的80多年里，按时间顺序播出的谈话类广播送来了不期而遇的收获，让开车途中或枯燥体力劳动中的某个时刻意外变得生动。我在自己那本关于广播的书中记下了一次开车时的经历：“我了解了最新新闻，学到了一些17世纪的历史，还意外地从一档朴实无华、介绍听诊器历史的节目中增长了见识。”"},{"en":"But radio's enriching serendipity is ebbing. With multiple networks and countless podcasts, a smartphone user selects what to hear and when. And while it is wonderful to take a walk with anything in your headphones, infinite choice encourages us to shrink into niche interests and sympathetic beliefs.","zh":"然而，广播这种丰富生活、带来意外收获的能力正在消退。面对众多广播网络和数不清的播客，智能手机用户可以选择听什么、何时听。虽然散步时能通过耳机收听任何想听的内容很美妙，但无限的选择也会促使我们退缩到狭窄的兴趣和与自己观点相投的信念之中。"},{"en":"A hundred years on from Marconi and Reith, is the art of mere listening endangered? Some will say the audiobook boom revives it, though I suppose you can then worry about the decline of reading. But inventions shape all of us and it is worth noticing when techno-social habits do change, and asking whether to control them a bit or shield the youngest. Whatever we do, innovation will happen. Today we fret about the isolating culture of smartphone-staring and selfie-vanity, but already in 1939 there was that lady regretting how, when all her street got wirelesses, it lost the neighbourly habit of “talking on the brush handle”. It's enough to make a person put down the smartphone and go out front with a yard broom.","zh":"从马可尼和里斯所处的时代算起，一百年过去了，单纯倾听的艺术是否正面临危险？有人会说，有声书的繁荣让这种艺术复兴了，不过我想，接着你又可以担心阅读的衰落。但发明塑造着我们每一个人。当与技术有关的社会习惯确实发生变化时，值得留意，并问一问是否应稍加控制，或保护年龄最小的孩子。无论我们怎么做，创新都会发生。今天，我们为盯着手机和热衷自拍所造成的孤立文化而忧虑；然而早在1939年，就有那位女士遗憾地说，整条街的人都有了收音机以后，街上便失去了“倚着扫帚柄聊天”的邻里习惯。这足以让人放下智能手机，拿着一把院子用的扫帚走到门外去。"}],"notes":[{"text":"Opening this week, a book and exhibition curated by Beatty Rubens at the Bodleian in Oxford records how radio changed everyday life from 1922 to 1939.","translation":"本周，一本书及一场由比蒂·鲁本斯策划的展览在牛津的博德利图书馆推出，记录了广播在1922年至1939年间如何改变日常生活。","hint":"主干是 a book and exhibition ... records ...；curated by ... 是过去分词短语，补充说明书与展览的策划者；how引导宾语从句，交代所记录的内容。Opening this week补充推出时间。"},{"text":"And while it is wonderful to take a walk with anything in your headphones, infinite choice encourages us to shrink into niche interests and sympathetic beliefs.","translation":"虽然散步时能通过耳机收听任何想听的内容很美妙，但无限的选择也会促使我们退缩到狭窄的兴趣和与自己观点相投的信念之中。","hint":"while在这里表示“虽然”，引导让步从句；主句是 infinite choice encourages us to shrink ...。从句中的it是形式主语，真正的主语是to take a walk ...；sympathetic在此表示观点相投，不是“值得同情的”。"},{"text":"But inventions shape all of us and it is worth noticing when techno-social habits do change, and asking whether to control them a bit or shield the youngest.","translation":"但发明塑造着我们每一个人；当与技术有关的社会习惯确实发生变化时，值得留意，并问一问是否应稍加控制，或保护年龄最小的孩子。","hint":"and连接两个主句：inventions shape ... 与 it is worth ...。worth后的noticing和asking并列；when从句说明留意什么或何时留意，do强调“确实”；whether to ...说明所思考的问题。the youngest指年龄最小的人，语境中是孩子。"}],"questions":[{"number":31,"type":"choice","stem":"What can be learnt about wireless from Sir John Reith?","stemZh":"从约翰·里斯爵士的话中，可以了解到无线广播的什么特点？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"It was accessible to everyone.","meaning":"人人都能收听它。accessible：能够接触或使用的；everyone：每个人。"},{"key":"B","text":"It improved interpersonal relations.","meaning":"它改善了人际关系。improve：改善；interpersonal relations：人际关系。"},{"key":"C","text":"It was a miracle of human ingenuity.","meaning":"它是人类才智创造的奇迹。miracle：奇迹；ingenuity：聪明才智、独创性。"},{"key":"D","text":"It led to a new era of isolating culture.","meaning":"它开启了孤立文化的新时代。lead to：导致、带来；era：时代；isolating：使人孤立的。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"里斯说天才与愚者、富人与穷人都能同时收听，强调广播人人可用，因此选 A。","points":2},{"number":32,"type":"choice","stem":"What is the theme of the exhibition at the Bodleian in Oxford?","stemZh":"牛津博德利图书馆这场展览的主题是什么？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The impact of radio on its early audience.","meaning":"广播对早期听众的影响。impact：影响；early audience：早期听众。"},{"key":"B","text":"The role of radio in public music education.","meaning":"广播在公众音乐教育中的作用。role：作用；public music education：公众音乐教育。"},{"key":"C","text":"The innovation process of radio technology.","meaning":"广播技术的创新过程。innovation：创新；process：过程。"},{"key":"D","text":"The eminent pioneers in radio broadcasting.","meaning":"广播事业中杰出的先驱人物。eminent：杰出的、著名的；pioneer：先驱。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"第二段明确说展览记录了1922至1939年间广播如何改变日常生活，A 对应早期听众所受的影响。","points":2},{"number":33,"type":"choice","stem":"It is indicated in Paragraph 4 that____","stemZh":"第4段表明了什么？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the research on radio used to be inadequate.","meaning":"过去有关广播的研究不充分。used to：过去曾经；inadequate：不足的、不充分的。"},{"key":"B","text":"the charm of radio remains in the digital age.","meaning":"广播的魅力在数字时代仍然存在。charm：魅力；remain：仍然存在；digital age：数字时代。"},{"key":"C","text":"radio listeners could make unexpected gains.","meaning":"广播听众可能获得意想不到的收获。unexpected：意料之外的；gain：收获。"},{"key":"D","text":"radio shows have changed little over the years.","meaning":"多年来，广播节目几乎没有变化。little：几乎没有；over the years：这些年来。"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"第四段提到 serendipity 和随机丰富旅途/劳动的经历，并举作者意外学到听诊器历史为例，C 对应意外收获。","points":2},{"number":34,"type":"choice","stem":"The expression “talking on the brush handle” in Paragraphs 3 and 6 refers to the act of____","stemZh":"第3段和第6段中“talking on the brush handle”这一表达指的是什么行为？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"making a loud noise.","meaning":"发出很大的噪声。make a loud noise：发出很响的声音。"},{"key":"B","text":"having a casual chat.","meaning":"随意闲聊。casual：随意的、非正式的；chat：闲聊。"},{"key":"C","text":"starting a trivial quarrel.","meaning":"为琐事吵起来。start：引起、开始；trivial：琐碎的；quarrel：争吵。"},{"key":"D","text":"humming a popular song.","meaning":"哼唱一首流行歌曲。hum：哼唱；popular song：流行歌曲。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段将它与街头交流减少相连，末段怀念邻里习惯；talking on the brush handle 是扶着扫帚闲聊，选 B。","points":2},{"number":35,"type":"choice","stem":"In the last paragraph, the author intends to express the opinion that____","stemZh":"作者在最后一段想表达什么观点？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"technology should be aimed at benefiting humans.","meaning":"技术应以造福人类为目标。be aimed at：旨在；benefit：使受益、造福。"},{"key":"B","text":"actions should be taken to revive the art of listening.","meaning":"应采取行动，让倾听的艺术复兴。take actions：采取行动；revive：使复兴。"},{"key":"C","text":"adolescents should form healthy social media habits.","meaning":"青少年应养成健康的社交媒体使用习惯。adolescent：青少年；form habits：养成习惯。"},{"key":"D","text":"people should adopt a sensible attitude to innovations.","meaning":"人们应以理性的态度对待创新。adopt：采取；sensible：明智的、理性的；attitude：态度。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"末段承认创新会继续，建议察觉习惯变化、适度控制并保护年轻人，体现理性对待创新；D 最全面。","points":2}],"image":null},{"id":"reading-4","title":"Reading · Text 4","subtitle":"36–40题 · 10分","type":"reading","directions":"Read the following text. Answer the questions after the text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET.","paragraphs":[{"en":"When Tom Swetnam joined the U.S. Forest Service in the 1970s, his mandate was to “put everything out,” he recalled. But when Swetnam enrolled in graduate school at the University of Arizona's Laboratory of Tree-Ring Research, he was surprised to find a record of repeated blazes dating back hundreds of years before European colonists arrived on the continent. Some of the trees he analyzed bore more than 20 fire scars among their rings.","zh":"汤姆·斯韦特纳姆回忆说，20世纪70年代他加入美国林务局时，接到的任务是“把所有火都扑灭”。但后来，他进入亚利桑那大学树轮研究实验室攻读研究生，惊讶地发现：在欧洲殖民者来到这片大陆之前的数百年里，这里就反复发生过火灾。他分析的一些树木，其年轮中留有20多处火疤。"},{"en":"The fact that fires happened so often meant they couldn't have been severe enough to kill most trees. Instead, a growing body of research showed that frequent, low-severity fires made many ecosystems healthier. They rid the forest of dead and sick trees, reducing competition and curbing the spread of disease. Because flammable material couldn't build up on the landscape, blazes tended to move slowly and peter out when they reached the footprints of previous burns.","zh":"火灾发生得如此频繁，意味着这些火不可能猛烈到足以杀死大多数树木。相反，越来越多的研究表明，频繁但烈度低的火灾会让许多生态系统更加健康。它们清除了林中的死树和病树，减少了竞争，并遏制疾病传播。由于可燃物无法在地表不断积累，火势往往蔓延缓慢，并在到达先前烧过的地段时逐渐熄灭。"},{"en":"In 2022, Swetnam and other scientists teamed up to compile a database of fire-scarred trees from across the continent. Their North American tree-ring fire-scar network (NAFSN) provided the basis for a study published last month. In the study, the researchers compared the historical fire cadence with the wildfires recorded over the past few decades, and uncovered a striking shortfall. The NAFSN sites experienced less than a quarter of the number of fires that would have been expected without fire suppression.","zh":"2022年，斯韦特纳姆与其他科学家合作，建立了一个收录北美大陆各地带有火疤树木的数据库。他们的“北美树轮火疤网络”（NAFSN）为上个月发表的一项研究提供了基础。在这项研究中，研究人员把历史上的火灾发生频率与过去几十年记录的野火进行比较，发现实际火灾次数明显不足。NAFSN各观测点经历的火灾次数，还不到假如没有人为灭火干预时预计会发生次数的四分之一。"},{"en":"This deficit is a testament to the effectiveness of modern firefighting, said Kelly Martin, a past president of the International Association of Wildland Fire. “Yet the combined consequences of suppression and climate change have eroded humanity's ability to suppress fires, particularly those that ignite under the most dangerous weather conditions.”","zh":"国际荒野火灾协会前主席凯莉·马丁说，这种火灾次数的减少证明了现代灭火工作的有效性。“然而，灭火与气候变化共同造成的后果，已经削弱了人类扑灭火灾的能力，尤其是扑灭那些在最危险天气条件下燃起的火灾的能力。”"},{"en":"To prevent entire ecosystems from going up in smoke, Martin said, people must bring healthy fire back to places that need it. At Yosemite National Park, Martin oversaw the use of what is known as prescribed burns to make the landscape more resilient. These fires were carefully planned and intentionally ignited during periods when weather kept the blazes easy to control, and helped eliminate some of the fuel that had built up around the important park's facilities. Research shows that these prescribed burns make subsequent wildfires less severe, even if later fires happen under the most dangerous weather conditions.","zh":"马丁说，要防止整个生态系统毁于大火，人们必须把有益的火带回需要它的地方。在优胜美地国家公园，马丁监督实施了所谓的“计划烧除”，以提高当地景观生态系统的恢复能力。这些火经过周密规划，在天气使火势容易控制的时期有意点燃，帮助清除公园重要设施周围已经积累的部分可燃物。研究表明，这些计划烧除能降低后续野火的烈度，即使后来的火灾发生在最危险的天气条件下也是如此。"},{"en":"Yet even as scientists and public officials increasingly agree on the need for more fires in our forests, climate change is making this tactic more challenging, experts said. “It's a double-edged sword because wildfires are getting more severe and larger under climate change and we need this work even more, but then the work gets more challenging,” said Susan Prichard, a fire ecologist at the University of Washington.","zh":"然而，专家说，尽管科学家和政府官员越来越认同森林中需要更多火灾，气候变化却使这种做法更具挑战性。华盛顿大学火生态学家苏珊·普里查德说：“这是一把双刃剑，因为气候变化使野火越来越猛烈、规模越来越大，我们因而更加需要这项工作；但与此同时，这项工作也变得更加困难。”"}],"notes":[{"text":"The fact that fires happened so often meant they couldn't have been severe enough to kill most trees.","translation":"火灾发生得如此频繁，意味着这些火不可能猛烈到足以杀死大多数树木。","hint":"主干是 The fact ... meant ...；that fires happened so often是同位语从句，说明fact的具体内容。couldn’t have been是对过去情况的否定推断；enough to kill ...说明严重程度达到的结果。"},{"text":"The NAFSN sites experienced less than a quarter of the number of fires that would have been expected without fire suppression.","translation":"NAFSN各观测点经历的火灾次数，还不到假如没有人为灭火干预时预计会发生次数的四分之一。","hint":"主干是 The NAFSN sites experienced less than a quarter of ...。that引导定语从句修饰the number of fires；without fire suppression暗含假设条件，would have been expected表示与实际历史不同的假设结果。比较的是实际次数与无干预时的预计次数。"},{"text":"Research shows that these prescribed burns make subsequent wildfires less severe, even if later fires happen under the most dangerous weather conditions.","translation":"研究表明，这些计划烧除能降低后续野火的烈度，即使后来的火灾发生在最危险的天气条件下也是如此。","hint":"主干是 Research shows that ...，that引导宾语从句。宾语从句中的make + 宾语 + 形容词表示“使……变得……”；even if引导让步条件从句。prescribed burns指事先规划、在可控条件下实施的烧除。"}],"questions":[{"number":36,"type":"choice","stem":"According to Paragraph 1, Swetnam was surprised by","stemZh":"根据第1段，什么使斯韦特纳姆感到惊讶？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the scarcity of tree-ring research in the U.S.","meaning":"美国的树轮研究十分匮乏。scarcity：缺乏、稀少；tree-ring：树轮、年轮。"},{"key":"B","text":"the firefighting measures in ancient Europe.","meaning":"古代欧洲的灭火措施。firefighting：灭火；measure：措施；ancient：古代的。"},{"key":"C","text":"the forest management practices in the 1970s.","meaning":"20世纪70年代的森林管理做法。forest management：森林管理；practice：做法、惯例。"},{"key":"D","text":"the number of wildfires in precolonial times.","meaning":"殖民时代之前野火发生的次数。number：数量、次数；precolonial：殖民时期以前的。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"第一段说他惊讶地发现，欧洲殖民者到来几百年前就有反复火灾记录，某些树有二十多处火痕，D 对应。","points":2},{"number":37,"type":"choice","stem":"Paragraph 2 mainly focuses on____","stemZh":"第2段主要关注什么？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the causes of previous burns.","meaning":"先前火灾的起因。cause：原因、起因；previous：以前的；burn：火烧、火灾。"},{"key":"B","text":"the treatment of diseased trees.","meaning":"对病树的处理。treatment：处理、治疗；diseased：患病的。"},{"key":"C","text":"the benefits of low-severity fires.","meaning":"低烈度火灾的好处。benefit：益处；low-severity：烈度较低的、破坏较轻的。"},{"key":"D","text":"the importance of forest ecosystems.","meaning":"森林生态系统的重要性。importance：重要性；ecosystem：生态系统。"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"第二段先提出频繁低强度火灾让生态系统更健康，随后解释清理病死树、减少竞争与燃料等好处，C 概括主旨。","points":2},{"number":38,"type":"choice","stem":"What did the study find about the wildfires over the past few decades?","stemZh":"这项研究对过去几十年中的野火有什么发现？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Their intensity has vastly fluctuated.","meaning":"其强度大幅波动。intensity：强度；vastly：大幅地；fluctuate：波动。"},{"key":"B","text":"Their frequency has markedly decreased.","meaning":"其发生频率明显下降。frequency：频率；markedly：明显地；decrease：减少、下降。"},{"key":"C","text":"Their threats have been underestimated.","meaning":"其威胁被低估了。threat：威胁；underestimate：低估。"},{"key":"D","text":"Their records have been misinterpreted.","meaning":"有关记录遭到了误读。record：记录；misinterpret：误解、错误解读。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"第三段说火灾数量不足无灭火干预时预期的四分之一，说明发生频次明显减少，选 B；并未说火灾强度降低。","points":2},{"number":39,"type":"choice","stem":"What can be inferred about modern firefighting?","stemZh":"关于现代灭火工作，可以推断出什么？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Its workforce needs more training.","meaning":"其工作人员需要接受更多培训。workforce：全体工作人员；training：培训。"},{"key":"B","text":"Its effectiveness is questioned by the public.","meaning":"其有效性受到公众质疑。effectiveness：有效性；question：质疑；the public：公众。"},{"key":"C","text":"It may render traditional tactics useless.","meaning":"它可能使传统方法失去作用。render：使处于某种状态；tactic：策略、方法；useless：无用的。"},{"key":"D","text":"It may make severe fires harder to put out.","meaning":"它可能使猛烈火灾更难扑灭。severe：严重的、猛烈的；put out：扑灭。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"第四段指出灭火与气候变化共同削弱人类扑灭火灾的能力，尤其是危险天气下的火灾，D 是合理推断。","points":2},{"number":40,"type":"choice","stem":"Both Martin and Prichard would agree that____","stemZh":"马丁和普里查德都会同意什么观点？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"it is challenging to predict large wildfires.","meaning":"预测大型野火具有挑战性。challenging：困难的、有挑战性的；predict：预测。"},{"key":"B","text":"it is urgent to assess the use of planned fires.","meaning":"评估有计划用火的运用已刻不容缓。urgent：迫切的；assess：评估；planned fire：有计划的用火。"},{"key":"C","text":"it is necessary to introduce prescribed burns.","meaning":"有必要引入计划烧除。necessary：必要的；introduce：引入、采用；prescribed burn：计划烧除。"},{"key":"D","text":"it is rewarding to double fire detection efforts.","meaning":"加倍投入火灾探测工作会带来回报。rewarding：有回报的、值得做的；double：使加倍；fire detection：火灾探测。"}],"answer":"C","explanation":"Martin 提倡让健康的火回归，Prichard 说这项工作更有必要但更困难，二人都赞成实施计划烧除，选 C。","points":2}],"image":null},{"id":"matching","title":"新题型 · 段落排序","subtitle":"41–45题 · 10分","type":"matching","directions":"The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For questions 41–45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A–H and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs F, H and C have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)\n原卷顺序框：F → 41 → 42 → H → 43 → C → 44 → 45。","paragraphs":[{"en":"A. And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","zh":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"en":"B. But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","zh":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"en":"C. Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","zh":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"en":"D. It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","zh":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"en":"E. We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","zh":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"en":"F. You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","zh":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"en":"G. Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","zh":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"en":"H. Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","zh":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"notes":[{"text":"F. You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","translation":"你不需要更多地出门；如果你像多数商界人士那样终日奔波，你真正需要的是多待在家中，坐下来读本书。","hint":"If…引导条件从句；主句的主语是 what you really need to do，谓语为 is，表语是省略 to 的动词短语 stay…sit…and read…。"},{"text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands.","translation":"研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿；如果手里有一本好书，我们会更享受长寿。","hint":"has been found 是被动语态；三个 to do 并列。破折号后 something 指 longevity，后接省略关系词的定语从句。"}],"questions":[{"number":41,"type":"choice","stem":"第 41 处应填入哪一段？","stemZh":"第 41 处应填入哪一段？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","meaning":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","meaning":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","meaning":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","meaning":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","meaning":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","meaning":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","meaning":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","meaning":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"answer":"B","explanation":"固定首段 F 建议商界人士坐下来读书；B 以 But 转折，this 回指该建议，指出现实中商界人士缺乏阅读。","points":2},{"number":42,"type":"choice","stem":"第 42 处应填入哪一段？","stemZh":"第 42 处应填入哪一段？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","meaning":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","meaning":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","meaning":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","meaning":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","meaning":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","meaning":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","meaning":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","meaning":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"answer":"E","explanation":"B 末尾以浩瀚的阅读海洋作引导；E 推荐科学读物，与后面固定段 H 推荐小说形成并列，golden age…也相互呼应。","points":2},{"number":43,"type":"choice","stem":"第 43 处应填入哪一段？","stemZh":"第 43 处应填入哪一段？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","meaning":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","meaning":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","meaning":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","meaning":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","meaning":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","meaning":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","meaning":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","meaning":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"answer":"A","explanation":"固定 H 推荐小说；A 以 And just read a single poem 继续建议读诗，随后固定 C 转向阅读益处的科学证据。","points":2},{"number":44,"type":"choice","stem":"第 44 处应填入哪一段？","stemZh":"第 44 处应填入哪一段？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","meaning":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","meaning":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","meaning":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","meaning":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","meaning":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","meaning":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","meaning":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","meaning":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"answer":"G","explanation":"固定 C 讲阅读对专注、社交理解和想象力的益处；G 的 also 补充助人、减少偏见和长寿等益处。","points":2},{"number":45,"type":"choice","stem":"第 45 处应填入哪一段？","stemZh":"第 45 处应填入哪一段？","options":[{"key":"A","text":"And just read a single poem. In his Oxford lectures, Seamus Heaney argued that a poem draws a picture of reality, a “glimpsed alternative” that sets up a contradiction with your own, in ways little and large. The negotiation, between you and it, is the heart of the matter. What does the poet see that you don't? What does the difference mean? It could be one of the best conversations you ever have. Forget self-help books; reading is self-help in action.","meaning":"A. 那就读一首诗吧。在牛津讲座中，谢默斯·希尼认为，一首诗会描画现实，为人提供一种“偶然瞥见的另一种可能”，它在大大小小的方面与你的现实形成矛盾。你与诗之间的协商，才是问题的核心。诗人看到了什么而你没有看到？这种差异意味着什么？这可能是你一生中最精彩的对话之一。忘掉自助书吧；阅读就是付诸实践的自我帮助。"},{"key":"B","text":"But for the most part, this isn't what the business community does. I have yet to meet a chief executive who reads regularly. Many skip newspapers, and magazines are a stretch. They don't have time, they say. It's inefficient; they can get the information they need from those around them. At a pinch, they might pick up a business book before a long flight, in the hope that, like a cookbook, it will provide a foolproof recipe. Some are drawn to what I think of as “business car crash” books—the stories of Theranos, Purdue, or WeWork. But outside those narrow pools of interest, a vast ocean awaits, bountiful with simmering ideas, mental adventure and imaginative refreshment.","meaning":"B. 然而，大多数时候，商界并不这样做。我还没有遇到哪位首席执行官有经常阅读的习惯。很多人连报纸也不看，更别说杂志了。他们说自己没有时间。阅读效率太低；他们可以从身边人那里获得所需信息。实在没有办法时，他们或许会在长途飞行前拿起一本商业书，指望它像食谱一样，提供一种万无一失的方法。有些人喜欢我所谓的“商业车祸”书——Theranos、Purdue 或 WeWork 的故事。但在这些狭窄的兴趣池塘之外，有一片浩瀚海洋正等着他们，里面满是酝酿中的思想、心智的冒险和想象力的新生。"},{"key":"C","text":"Neuroscientists have been at pains to demonstrate that the pleasure a book provides isn't indulgence; it's good for you. Reading will keep you better informed about the world but it can also improve our tech-shattered ability to concentrate. Standing in the shoes of others fine tunes our social understanding, useful as we struggle to understand friends, neighbours, customers and co-workers. Different parts of our brain engage as we simulate scenes, characters and mental states. Our imagination—remember that?—is rekindled.","meaning":"C. 神经科学家一直竭力证明，书籍带来的快乐并非放纵；阅读对你有益。阅读使你更了解世界，还可以改善我们被科技打得支离破碎的专注能力。设身处地站在他人的立场，能微调我们的社会理解力，这有助于我们努力理解朋友、邻居、客户和同事。当我们模拟场景、人物和心理状态时，大脑的不同部位会参与其中。我们的想象力——还记得它吗？——重新被点燃了。"},{"key":"D","text":"It is undoubtedly true that all work and no play really does make Jack, or Jill, dull. The cure is right at hand. Reading is cheap, easy and, most important, it's fun. Liberate your imagination this year.","meaning":"D. 毋庸置疑，只工作不玩耍，真的会让杰克或者吉尔变得乏味。良方就在手边。阅读便宜、容易，而且最重要的是，它充满乐趣。今年就释放你的想象力吧。"},{"key":"E","text":"We are living through a golden age of science writing, so lucid and accessible that even lay readers can relish the unpredictability of discovery. Daunted by uncertainty? Stand in the shoes of scientists and witness the degree to which breakthroughs emerge from accidents, conflict and sheer mental stamina. “We are never sure of anything,” says the physicist (and writer) Carlo Rovelli.","meaning":"E. 我们正处于科学写作的黄金时代，作品清晰易懂，甚至外行读者也能享受发现过程的不可预测性。害怕不确定性吗？站在科学家的立场，看看突破有多大程度来自偶然、冲突和纯粹的精神毅力。物理学家兼作家卡洛·罗韦利说：“我们从来无法对任何事情完全确定。”"},{"key":"F","text":"You don't need to get out more. If, like most business people, you spend your life dashing from office to plane, train to home, boardroom to washroom, what you really need to do is stay in more, sit down—and read a book.","meaning":"F. 你不需要更多地出门。如果你像多数商界人士那样，一生都在办公室与飞机、火车与家、会议室与洗手间之间奔波，那么你真正需要的，是多待在家里，坐下来——读一本书。"},{"key":"G","text":"Reading has also been found to make us more helpful, to reduce bias, and even to increase longevity—something we will enjoy all the more if we have a good book in our hands. (And yes, all these benefits are more closely associated with physical books than digital ones.)","meaning":"G. 研究还发现，阅读能使我们更乐于助人、减少偏见，甚至延年益寿——如果手里有一本好书，我们对长寿也会享受得更多。（没错，所有这些好处与纸质书的联系，都比与电子书的联系更紧密。）"},{"key":"H","text":"Read fiction. Any fiction. Free yourself from algorithms and choose—anything. You don't need technology for an immersive experience—just surrender to · narratives across time and place. Modern (Sebastian Barry or Olga Tokarczuk), classic (Virginia Woolf or James Baldwin) or genre (Stephen King, Margaret Atwood, Georges Simenon) —it doesn't matter. Fiction invites you to loiter unseen in the lives of others. We are living through a golden age of translation too, so you can go anywhere in time or place.","meaning":"H. 读小说吧。什么小说都可以。从算法中解放自己，选择——任何一本。你不需要技术就能获得沉浸式体验，只需投入跨越时空的叙事。现代作品（塞巴斯蒂安·巴里或奥尔加·托卡尔丘克）、经典作品（弗吉尼亚·伍尔夫或詹姆斯·鲍德温），或者类型作品（斯蒂芬·金、玛格丽特·阿特伍德、乔治·西默农）——都无所谓。小说邀请你悄悄流连于他人的生活。我们也正处于翻译的黄金时代，所以你可以去往任何时间和地点。"}],"answer":"D","explanation":"D 总结只工作不休闲的问题，强调阅读便宜、容易、有趣，并发出行动号召，适合作结尾。","points":2}],"image":null,"sequence":["F",41,42,"H",43,"C",44,45]},{"id":"translation","title":"英译汉","subtitle":"46–50题 · 10分","type":"translation","directions":"Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)","paragraphs":[{"en":"Science education today revolves around the idea of scientific literacy—the base-level knowledge about science that nonscientists require to effectively get on in the world. This concept has served as a central goal for curriculum developers, local school boards, business and community leaders, and policymakers ever since its introduction nearly 80 years ago.","zh":"如今，科学教育围绕“科学素养”这一理念展开，即非科学工作者为了有效应对现实生活而需要具备的基本科学知识。这个概念自近八十年前提出以来，就一直是课程开发者、地方学校董事会、商界与社区领袖以及政策制定者的核心目标。"},{"en":"(46) Tracing the history of the term, we can see how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time, muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education. And that's a shame, because there is much to recommend in the idea of scientific literacy as it was originally articulated in 1945, a time when science appeared to be the key to progress and scientists seemingly held the fate of the world in their hands. (47) A return to that version of scientific literacy, which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts, seems like something society today desperately needs.","zh":"（46）追溯这个术语的历史，我们就能看到科学素养的定义如何随时间发生变化，而在确定科学教育的目标时，这种变化又使问题变得模糊不清。这令人遗憾，因为一九四五年最初提出科学素养理念时，其中有许多可取之处；那时，科学似乎是进步的关键，而科学家仿佛掌握着世界的命运。（47）回归那种科学素养理念，也就是更重视教授科学是什么、如何运作，而较少强调记忆科学事实的理念，似乎是当今社会迫切需要的。"},{"en":"In the United States, the desire to provide the public with a general, nontechnical education in science originated as far back as the late 1800s. (48) Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills. The development in 1915 of the popular new subject “general science” was another effort to train students to apply the principles of science to everyday, nonscience problems.","zh":"在美国，向公众提供一般性、非专业的科学教育的愿望，最早可以追溯到十九世纪末。（48）教育工作者提出，让高中生完成详尽的实验室练习，因为他们认为这类工作主要有利于增强逻辑推理与观察能力。一九一五年广受欢迎的新学科“普通科学”的发展，则是又一次尝试，旨在训练学生将科学原理应用于日常的非科学问题。"},{"en":"Although these efforts were aimed at the nonscience-bound student, they never really made their way into mainstream thought and public discourse as a means to rally widespread support for the importance of science teaching in schools. (49) It wasn't until the phrase “scientific literacy” came along in the 1940s that science had the formidable slogan it needed to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal.","zh":"尽管这些努力面向的是未来不从事科学工作的学生，但它们始终没有真正进入主流思想和公共讨论，成为争取公众广泛支持学校科学教育重要性的一种方式。（49）直到二十世纪四十年代“科学素养”这一说法出现，科学才拥有了吸引公众关注所需要的响亮口号，并使改进科学教育成为重要的国家目标。"},{"en":"(50) The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War I, as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers. As the war unfolded, science rapidly assumed a central role. Battles increasingly depended on new military technologies such as radar and the proximity fuze. Science-based analytical approaches proved remarkably successful in the hunt for German submarines in the Atlantic Ocean. And there was the (then-secret) work building the world's first atomic bomb. As a result, scientists— physicists in particular—found themselves in high demand.","zh":"（50）美国最初高度重视科学素养，源于科学技术在第一次世界大战期间发挥的关键作用，以及人们所认为的美国士兵存在的不足。随着战争展开，科学迅速处于核心地位。战斗越来越依赖雷达和近炸引信等新军事技术。在大西洋追踪德国潜艇时，基于科学的分析方法被证明极为成功。此外，还有当时处于保密状态的研制世界第一颗原子弹的工作。因此，科学家——尤其是物理学家——变得十分抢手。【资料提示：用户提供的原卷第50题印为 World War I，但后文雷达、原子弹等例子涉及二战；此处忠实保留原卷表述。】"}],"notes":[{"text":"Tracing the history of the term, we can see how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time, muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education.","translation":"追溯这个术语的历史，我们可以看到，科学素养的定义是如何随着时间推移而变化的，而这也使确定科学教育目标的问题变得模糊不清。","hint":"Tracing…是分词结构，主句为 we can see how…；how 引出宾语从句。muddying the waters 是比喻，意为“使问题模糊不清”，when it comes to…译为“在谈到/涉及……时”。"},{"text":"A return to that version of scientific literacy, which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts, seems like something society today desperately needs.","translation":"回归那种更注重教授科学的本质及其运作方式、而较少强调记忆科学事实的科学素养理念，似乎是当今社会迫切需要的。","hint":"主干 A return…seems like something…；which 引导非限制性定语从句，说明旧版本理念；more…less…对照的是教学侧重点。society today desperately needs 修饰 something。"},{"text":"Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills.","translation":"教育工作者提出，应让高中生完成详尽的实验室练习，因为他们相信这类活动的益处主要在于增强逻辑推理能力和观察能力。","hint":"主干 Educators advanced the idea…；of having students complete…解释该主张的内容，have sb do 意为“让某人做”。in the belief that…解释提出主张的理由。"},{"text":"It wasn't until the phrase “scientific literacy” came along in the 1940s that science had the formidable slogan it needed to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal.","translation":"直到二十世纪四十年代“科学素养”这一说法出现，科学才拥有了吸引公众注意所需要的响亮口号，并使改善科学教育成为一项重要的国家目标。","hint":"It wasn’t until…that…是“直到……才……”强调句。it needed 修饰 slogan，to command…and make…说明 slogan 的作用。make 后为“宾语＋宾补”。"},{"text":"The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War I, as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers.","translation":"美国最初对科学素养的高度重视，源于科学技术在第一次世界大战中发挥的关键作用，以及人们所认为的美国士兵存在的不足。","hint":"主干 The intense focus…grew out of…，grew out of 表示“源于”；as well as 连接两个原因。perceived 表示“被认为的”，不是证实存在的。【原卷印 World War I，原文与后文二战事例存在不一致，保留原卷。】"}],"questions":[{"number":46,"type":"text","stem":"Tracing the history of the term, we can see how the definition of scientific literacy has shifted over time, muddying the waters when it comes to determining the goals of science education.","stemZh":"请将这一段画线句译成中文。","options":[],"answer":"追溯这个术语的历史，我们可以看到，科学素养的定义是如何随着时间推移而变化的，而这也使确定科学教育目标的问题变得模糊不清。","explanation":"Tracing…是分词结构，主句为 we can see how…；how 引出宾语从句。muddying the waters 是比喻，意为“使问题模糊不清”，when it comes to…译为“在谈到/涉及……时”。","points":2},{"number":47,"type":"text","stem":"A return to that version of scientific literacy, which focused more on teaching what science is and how it works and less on memorizing scientific facts, seems like something society today desperately needs.","stemZh":"请将这一段画线句译成中文。","options":[],"answer":"回归那种更注重教授科学的本质及其运作方式、而较少强调记忆科学事实的科学素养理念，似乎是当今社会迫切需要的。","explanation":"主干 A return…seems like something…；which 引导非限制性定语从句，说明旧版本理念；more…less…对照的是教学侧重点。society today desperately needs 修饰 something。","points":2},{"number":48,"type":"text","stem":"Educators advanced the idea of having students complete detailed laboratory exercises in high schools in the belief that such work was beneficial primarily as a way to enhance logical reasoning and observational skills.","stemZh":"请将这一段画线句译成中文。","options":[],"answer":"教育工作者提出，应让高中生完成详尽的实验室练习，因为他们相信这类活动的益处主要在于增强逻辑推理能力和观察能力。","explanation":"主干 Educators advanced the idea…；of having students complete…解释该主张的内容，have sb do 意为“让某人做”。in the belief that…解释提出主张的理由。","points":2},{"number":49,"type":"text","stem":"It wasn't until the phrase “scientific literacy” came along in the 1940s that science had the formidable slogan it needed to command public attention and make improving science education an important national goal.","stemZh":"请将这一段画线句译成中文。","options":[],"answer":"直到二十世纪四十年代“科学素养”这一说法出现，科学才拥有了吸引公众注意所需要的响亮口号，并使改善科学教育成为一项重要的国家目标。","explanation":"It wasn’t until…that…是“直到……才……”强调句。it needed 修饰 slogan，to command…and make…说明 slogan 的作用。make 后为“宾语＋宾补”。","points":2},{"number":50,"type":"text","stem":"The intense focus on scientific literacy in the United States originally grew out of the critical role of science and technology during World War I, as well as the perceived deficiencies of American soldiers.","stemZh":"请将这一段画线句译成中文。","options":[],"answer":"美国最初对科学素养的高度重视，源于科学技术在第一次世界大战中发挥的关键作用，以及人们所认为的美国士兵存在的不足。","explanation":"主干 The intense focus…grew out of…，grew out of 表示“源于”；as well as 连接两个原因。perceived 表示“被认为的”，不是证实存在的。【原卷印 World War I，原文与后文二战事例存在不一致，保留原卷。】","points":2}],"image":null},{"id":"writing-a","title":"写作 · 小作文","subtitle":"51题 · 10分","type":"writing","directions":"Read the following email from your friend Paul and write him a reply. Hi Li Ming, I was really moved by the Chinese families' handwritten letters you posted yesterday. They are priceless! Could you please tell me a bit more about them? And are they currently on public display somewhere? I’m very keen to see them in person. Thanks. Yours, Paul You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email; use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","paragraphs":[{"en":"Read the following email from your friend Paul and write him a reply. Hi Li Ming, I was really moved by the Chinese families' handwritten letters you posted yesterday. They are priceless! Could you please tell me a bit more about them? And are they currently on public display somewhere? I’m very keen to see them in person. Thanks. Yours, Paul You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email; use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","zh":"阅读朋友保罗发来的邮件，并给他回信。保罗说：“李明，你好！你昨天发布的中国家庭手写家书让我十分感动。它们太珍贵了！你能再介绍一些吗？这些家书现在是否在某处公开展出？我很想亲眼看看。谢谢！保罗。”请在答题卡上写约100词。邮件中不得使用自己的姓名，使用“Li Ming”。（10分）"}],"notes":[],"questions":[{"number":51,"type":"text","stem":"Read the following email from your friend Paul and write him a reply. Hi Li Ming, I was really moved by the Chinese families' handwritten letters you posted yesterday. They are priceless! Could you please tell me a bit more about them? And are they currently on public display somewhere? I’m very keen to see them in person. Thanks. Yours, Paul You should write about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in the email; use “Li Ming” instead. (10 points)","stemZh":"给朋友保罗回信，介绍中国家书并回答是否公开展出。约100词，署名 Li Ming。","options":[],"answer":"Dear Paul,\n\nI am glad that you enjoyed the handwritten family letters I posted. They were written by Chinese people working or studying far from home. Besides sharing news, the writers expressed their love for their families and hopes for the future. These ordinary words make the letters precious records of family life.\n\nSome of the letters are now on public display at our local museum. The exhibition is open from Tuesday to Sunday, and admission is free. If you are available, we could visit it together this weekend.\n\nBest regards,\nLi Ming","explanation":"本范文由模型按题意编写，供练习参考，不是唯一标准答案。重点答全两个问题：介绍家书；说明展出地点/情况。博物馆和开放时间为写作中合理虚构的信息，不是现实展览通知。语气为朋友间回信，开头回应、正文说明、结尾自然邀请，署名必须 Li Ming。","points":10}],"image":null},{"id":"writing-b","title":"写作 · 大作文","subtitle":"52题 · 20分","type":"writing","directions":"Write an essay based on the charts below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the charts briefly, 2) interpret the charts, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points) 一项关于养老机器人的消费者接受度和首要关注点的调查","paragraphs":[{"en":"Write an essay based on the charts below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the charts briefly, 2) interpret the charts, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points) 一项关于养老机器人的消费者接受度和首要关注点的调查","zh":"根据下列图表写一篇短文。文中应：1）简要描述图表；2）解释图表；3）发表你的评论。请在答题卡上写160—200词。（20分）图表标题：一项关于养老机器人的消费者接受度和首要关注点的调查。"}],"notes":[],"questions":[{"number":52,"type":"text","stem":"Write an essay based on the charts below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the charts briefly, 2) interpret the charts, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points) 一项关于养老机器人的消费者接受度和首要关注点的调查","stemZh":"根据养老机器人调查图表写160—200词短文，包含描述、解释与评论。","options":[],"answer":"The two charts present consumers’ acceptance of elderly-care robots and their primary concerns. While 39.3% fully accept such robots and 32.8% accept them partially, 27.9% reject them. Safety is the leading concern, at 46.3%, followed by price at 24.9% and convenience at 10.7%. These figures suggest an attitude of interest mixed with caution.\n\nSeveral factors may explain this pattern. An aging population and busy working lives create a growing demand for assistance with daily care. Robots could remind older people to take medicine, help with routine tasks and alert family members in emergencies. However, caring for vulnerable people requires dependable technology. Consumers naturally worry about mechanical failures, privacy and the ability of machines to respond appropriately. The cost can also prevent ordinary families from adopting these products.\n\nIn my view, elderly-care robots can be useful partners in care. Manufacturers should put safety first, offer clear evidence from testing and work to make reliable products affordable. Meanwhile, families should remember that technology can support practical care, while personal attention and emotional companionship remain essential.","explanation":"本范文由模型编写，供学习参考，不是唯一标准答案。首段准确描述：完全接受39.3%、部分接受32.8%、不接受27.9%；首要关注安全46.3%、价格24.9%、便利10.7%。柱状图只列出三个关注点，合计不为100%，不能据此补写未知项目。第二段解释需求与顾虑，第三段评论安全、价格及人文关怀。","points":20}],"image":"./exam-assets/2026-writing-charts.png"}]}]};
